# Preset policy for this platform's game timers (ADR-044, backlog 2.4, D13-eng).
#
# Installed to /etc/systemd/system-preset/ by deploy.sh. This is NOT a unit
# file: systemd reads it only when someone runs `systemctl preset` or
# `systemctl preset-all`, never at boot on an installed system and never
# during daemon-reload. It changes nothing on its own.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS
# Without a matching rule, a unit falls through to systemd's built-in *enable*
# default. Every game timer here carries [Install] WantedBy=timers.target, so
# before this file `systemctl preset-all` would have switched on 15 disabled
# timers at once — four of them worldcup-*, which mail real players about a
# tournament that concluded 2026-07-19. All four also set Persistent=true, so
# an accidental enable fires IMMEDIATELY on the next start rather than waiting
# for the next scheduled tick. The defence used to be prose in two documents
# saying "never run preset-all", which is the remember-not-to-do-the-thing
# posture ADR-040 and ADR-041 exist to replace.
#
# WHY THE 10- PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING
# systemd.preset(5): "All preset files are sorted by their filename in
# lexicographic order, regardless of which of the directories they reside in"
# and "If multiple lines apply to a unit name, the first matching one takes
# precedence over all others." The box carries /usr/lib/systemd/system-preset/
# 90-systemd.preset, so 10- is read first and wins. Do not rename this to
# drop the prefix: 'f' sorts AFTER '9', which would put an unprefixed
# fantasy-platform.preset behind the vendor file. That is harmless only while
# 90-systemd.preset has no rule matching ours, which is not a property to
# depend on. tests/test_systemd_preset.py locks the prefix below 90.
#
# !! `systemctl preset-all --dry-run` IS NOT A DRY RUN. Never run it.
# systemd accepts --dry-run and silently ignores it for preset/preset-all (only
# verbs like reboot honour it). Run on the droplet 2026-08-13 it enabled 15 game
# timers for real, and wrote its output to stderr so `> file` looked empty.
# To inspect safely — no sudo, no writes, and it never pages:
#     systemctl list-unit-files 'worldcup-*.timer' 'cfb-*.timer' \
#                               'docket-*.timer' 'golf-*.timer' --no-pager
#
# READ BOTH COLUMNS. They answer different questions, and the 2026-08-13
# incident is exactly the case where they disagree:
#   PRESET = what this file says policy *should* be. Expect `disabled` for
#            worldcup-*, `ignored` for the three active games. It says nothing
#            about what is running.
#   STATE  = what is actually enabled right now. This is the one that matters.
#            Expect `enabled` for docket-{lines,scores,deadline,remind} only,
#            and `disabled` for everything else — including docket-setup.timer,
#            which stays disabled until after the hand-run Week-1 import
#            (Tue Sep 1) by ruling.
# During the incident PRESET read `ignored` while STATE read `enabled` on 15
# timers: a PRESET-only check would have reported all-clear with the hazard live.
#
# Recovery from an accidental preset-all is `systemctl disable --now` naming each
# unit. `ignore` cannot do it for you — it preserves whatever state it finds
# rather than asserting one, so it will happily preserve a wrong one.

# NO CATCH-ALL, EVER
# A `disable *` line here would outrank 90-systemd.preset for EVERY unit on
# the box — getty@.service, systemd-resolved.service, remote-fs.target. The
# validator in deploy.sh refuses any pattern not ending in .timer, which makes
# that class of mistake unrepresentable rather than merely discouraged.

# The World Cup is archived (2026 tournament concluded 2026-07-19; registry
# status 'completed'). Its units stay in deploy/ on purpose — the transition
# plan keeps them as the revival recipe, and deleting them would strand the
# /etc copies anyway, since deploy.sh does not remove orphans (ADR-041). So
# the assertion belongs here instead: these must never run again as things
# stand. `disable` rather than `ignore` because this is a claim about the
# world, not a shrug — if one is ever enabled by accident, preset-all is
# entitled to switch it back off. This line never needs revisiting.
disable worldcup-*.timer

# The three active games are hand-managed ops state, enabled one unit at a
# time at each game's milestone (`systemctl enable` takes no globs — enable by
# explicit name). `ignore` means preset-all leaves whatever is currently set
# alone: it can neither switch these on early nor switch a live one off.
#
# That second half is why these are not `disable`. Four docket timers
# (lines, scores, deadline, remind) are enabled and firing right now, and the
# five cfb-* join them at launch week. A `disable` line would make preset-all
# silently stop in-season reminders — trading one quiet footgun for another
# rather than removing it. `ignore` also costs nothing at a milestone, which
# `disable` would: it would need a deliberate flip per game, and a policy file
# that drifts from reality with no signal is worse than no policy file.
ignore cfb-*.timer
ignore docket-*.timer
ignore golf-*.timer

# A new game's timers are NOT covered until a line lands here, and would
# inherit the enable default. tests/test_systemd_preset.py fails CI when a
# prefix in deploy/*.timer has no rule, so that choice has to be made
# deliberately rather than inherited by silence — which is how the exposure
# went 4 -> 14 -> 19 with nobody widening it on purpose.
